s directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f82ff6ec76ab3cf26d2da516b6bf5c5d9773824043a38a26c8db8a16150bfc86.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-339711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "efe3194dee45cd632f12dbb8b74a7e7c91cc152570ce123c26a02a83fbbf630a"} {"question" "q-339711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-339711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-339711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+6y+9?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+6y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=-3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/074ed04c41f20a891678d61a26ae06f1151299110dfd13d2c8b7f6eef3e6e363.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "efe3194dee45cd632f12dbb8b74a7e7c91cc152570ce123c26a02a83fbbf630a"} {"question" "q-98763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$y=-3$ is a node"] ["text" "$y=-3$ is a node and $y=3$ is a sink"] ["text" "$y=-3$ is a node and $y=3$ is a source"] ["text" "$y=-3$ is a sink"] ["text" "$y=-3$ is a source"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$y=-3$ is a node"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=2+y-{y}^{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2+y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-y-2) & =0 \\\\ -(y+1)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/9230d847daa518edfaad43f6552a48c2b3cac9523a1e9ecad8624851080e00ea.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "efe3194dee45cd632f12dbb8b74a7e7c91cc152570ce123c26a02a83fbbf630a"} {"question" "q-98762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$y=-1$ and $y=2$ are both sinks"] ["text" "$y=-1$ and $y=2$ are both sources"] ["text" "$y=-1$ is a sink, and $y=2$ is a source"] ["text" "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node"] ["text" "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=-(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)(y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=3.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3),$ and negative for $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/3c118c900486d8561864c88e1b25a461c8f459f810bb7b753d30d9d0d4780913.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-3$  is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "efe3194dee45cd632f12dbb8b74a7e7c91cc152570ce123c26a02a83fbbf630a"} {"question" "q-98760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink"] ["text" "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a source"] ["text" "$y=-3$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node"] ["text" "$y=-3$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source"] ["text" "$y=-3$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-340352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+y-12?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-340352" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-12 & =0 \\\\ (y+4)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-4$ and $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-4,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/c2bb2cbc11afb9bb2b25fb2105d19c2748541592c77bcecab342db69834b4fe2.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source\"." "cate