se_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y+2)({y}^{2}+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=-2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/ac2d3df6e9caa672e16f3499502bba9e17c5d79dd200055319c22bc29fb78340.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-2$ is a source\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "efe3194dee45cd632f12dbb8b74a7e7c91cc152570ce123c26a02a83fbbf630a"} {"question" "q-98764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$y=-2$ is a node"] ["text" "$y=-2$ is a sink"] ["text" "$y=-2$ is a sink and $y=2$ is a source"] ["text" "$y=-2$ is a source"] ["text" "$y=-2$ is a source and $y=2$ is a sink"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$y=-2$ is a source"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/e5ba765b9caffab904f07c5086d3206f72e36e66cb018673ce7ed44d79d19e88.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to converge toward this line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "efe3194dee45cd632f12dbb8b74a7e7c91cc152570ce123c26a02a83fbbf630a"} {"question" "q-98774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/31519aa878fd6a9b450296552afd9bbf628e30db68a2d7c6dc6b02a48ae28b06.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/a375a537b104f5bcdd8f5951277bce2494d8861a7212772a7d7050fa14091cb9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/c7895d3f3db254736c4e20ff30dbe92fb7c0786291b1198ed3f727971268be31.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/d72110682c1959f10546b6fa49d6e4ebedf6b6d5557639c772f28a920f306df5.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points upward, the arrow just below it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5ea8af3b30df51189c3511dbe093f05b309dc50cbdea1e52d5ba3a42d88101c2.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "efe3194dee45cd632f12dbb8b74a7e7c91cc152570ce123c26a02a83fbbf630a"} {"question" "q-98809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/4e360e24357a714978fdfb64b239381b3d7c66d13a1307c265f067881aaebc54.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/7df58db225ecb32e6b51b2c16d78a557ca57e63f5d85551dfc1ddc85865e9ea4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Deve