9101292914032885458730bfaabf42e75a9447f43a1e7409c15fb8cbbaef.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-96557" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f3789101292914032885458730bfaabf42e75a9447f43a1e7409c15fb8cbbaef.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink\n\n- $y=2$ is a source\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is also a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/fb27ad885fc87fa56a04ec92d46173c98fda170f11117c4748c21a67faa9e166.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "efe3194dee45cd632f12dbb8b74a7e7c91cc152570ce123c26a02a83fbbf630a"} {"question" "q-98811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/0ffd362ca3f1e77749bf3a7aef59bb3891d58e22403c7c1fbda24c3dade769a6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/2348d561277c9f7ef89f057d7d2a1322ee2edcefb91e330cee9e9d7755879778.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/374c9e2680630f635ec736c30e66502b17ec935d0f540b990e835ac56922fe06.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/dffa0435b30afa7e39a4559b9069cd6f7f2312f2e97e2492fcd96bd6571ed6d5.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/fb27ad885fc87fa56a04ec92d46173c98fda170f11117c4748c21a67faa9e166.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "q-98811" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/fb27ad885fc87fa56a04ec92d46173c98fda170f11117c4748c21a67faa9e166.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/state.json" "file_sha256" "83068dd279f9700418ab112c81f1a154e905bf991fa628cbdb13bc365339a79b"} {"question" "e-8650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f495896e6df702a60eea6825d7d72c31e978d92d68ec7b1779fe565a8dc9b946.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/example-8650.json" "file_sha256" "9002dbf68ad8c3ae53b78c92fbd83496f17c605197f83e06cfed1efad184d1d9"} {"question" "e-8650" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=1$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=1.$ This means that $y = 1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=-2.$ This means that $y=-2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/example-8650.json" "file_sha256" "9002dbf68ad8c3ae53b78c92fbd83496f17c605197f83e06cfed1efad184d1d9"} {"question" "e-8538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/87dbc31b08ad8735b7a8e9f18bc74c448a221fe665acca3d40208786f20459ce.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y= -3$ is a sink.\n\n- $y= -1$ is a node.\n\n- $y= 2$ is a source." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/example-8538.json" "file_sha256" "22f4fd740378d944f094f529465edceed32e4ac0490d91cbf7652b4a73e0507f"} {"question" "e-8538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/413ddd3248fb53bbd623794970ee2b4a561d17e2659bb72cf864436d9fffa792.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/example-8538.json" "file_sha256" "22f4fd740378d944f094f529465edceed32e4ac0490d91cbf7652b4a73e0507f"} {"question" "e-8536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of $y'= y^3-2y^2?$ \n\n- $y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node\n\n- $y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\n\n- $y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a sink" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/example-8536.json" "file_sha256" "606503bb105f06ace8b456142f378087855d3940700db6c8383b070e2cbe4606"} {"question" "e-8536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/420aaceb6f78b8a6aa55f8e0a2a9a87e604875180a9321ff34bd72ca2384ec09.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/example-8536.json" "file_sha256" "606503bb105f06ace8b456142f378087855d3940700db6c8383b070e2cbe4606"} {"question" "e-8537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y= -2$ is a source\n\n- $y= 3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/07fcd4a3db9ed24d7e0c391095e5950c11be4b5dac2dcc5b4cda229d69f2aaa4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/example-8537.json" "file_sha256" "a2e68549bf9fad049602ae25dbd2bafd518c4664ff6e4de028c087cace8b1ced"} {"question" "e-8537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/0af6403f55ca8193993fee474183676ad4edfb0b99b4bf4707993ff512700358.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/3faccf2319817824a473b127ee7eff831ae7de82d9198b541b4d89dd7ef58ec2.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/example-8537.json" "file_sha256" "a2e68549bf9fad049602ae25dbd2bafd518c4664ff6e4de028c087cace8b1ced"}] "usage" {"e-8536" [] "e-8537" [] "q-98715" [] "q-96557" [] "q-96558" [] "q-98763" [] "q-98811" [] "e-8538" [] "e-8650" [] "q-339709" [] "q-98700" [] "q-98760" [] "q-98752" [] "q-339664" [] "q-98774" []} "basis" 2639 "retractions" [[17592186318142 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/528bf1bc9d6177f031b8b5504641903a11dfc2c2576e4759bf4cb7c278d0585c.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink\n- $y=3$ is a source\n- $y=3$ is a node"] [17592186148169 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/quest